# B03: Arithmetic Expression Evaluator & Clamping Limiter (`ExprEvaluator`)

This module implements an arithmetic expression evaluator and clamping limiter supporting addition (`op_code == 1`), subtraction (`op_code == 2`), multiplication (`op_code == 3`), and integer division (`op_code == 4`).
First-class contracts intercept division by zero (`DivisionByZero`) and invalid opcodes (`InvalidOpCode`), while guaranteeing that clamped outputs fall strictly within the closed interval `[min_bound, max_bound]`.

```jenny
module expr_evaluator

pub enum EvalError {
    DivisionByZero,
    InvalidOpCode,
    InvalidBounds,
}

pub struct EvalResult {
    pub raw_value: i64,
    pub clamped_value: i64,
    pub min_bound: i64,
    pub max_bound: i64,

    invariant {
        self.min_bound <= self.max_bound
        self.clamped_value >= self.min_bound
        self.clamped_value <= self.max_bound
    }
}

pub fn eval_binary(op_code: i32, lhs: i64, rhs: i64) -> i64 ! EvalError
    requires op_code >= 1 && op_code <= 4 else EvalError.InvalidOpCode
    requires op_code != 4 || rhs != 0 else EvalError.DivisionByZero

pub fn eval_and_clamp(
    op_code: i32,
    lhs: i64,
    rhs: i64,
    min_bound: i64,
    max_bound: i64
) -> EvalResult ! EvalError
    requires min_bound <= max_bound else EvalError.InvalidBounds
    ensures result.clamped_value >= min_bound
    ensures result.clamped_value <= max_bound

@example("EX-B03-01: arithmetic evaluation and clamping invariants") {
    val sum = eval_binary(1, 40, 2)!
    assert(sum == 42)

    val quot = eval_binary(4, 100, 5)!
    assert(quot == 20)

    val clamped = eval_and_clamp(3, 50, 10, 0, 255)!
    assert(clamped.raw_value == 500)
    assert(clamped.clamped_value == 255)
}

@example("EX-B03-02: division by zero and invalid opcode errors") {
    val div_zero = eval_binary(4, 10, 0)
    assert(div_zero is EvalError.DivisionByZero)

    val bad_op = eval_binary(99, 1, 2)
    assert(bad_op is EvalError.InvalidOpCode)

    val bad_bounds = eval_and_clamp(1, 10, 20, 100, 50)
    assert(bad_bounds is EvalError.InvalidBounds)
}
```
